  "image_name": "8ce900fee2813f5a030b097485f61fb0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a molecule, shown here as caffeine, can be represented as a graph for machine learning and then processed by a graph neural network. In the top portion, atoms are treated as nodes and bonds as edges. Different colors indicate atom types such as carbon, oxygen, nitrogen, and hydrogen, while single and double bonds are distinguished by different line styles. The caffeine structure is paired with common graph encodings: an adjacency matrix describing which atoms are connected, an edge list giving bond connections, and node and edge feature vectors that store atom identities and bond types numerically. A small legend links the visual chemistry to these labels.\n\nThe lower portion explains neural message passing on such a graph. A central node receives information from neighboring nodes through directed messages along edges. Each neighbor has its own node feature and each connecting bond has an edge feature, and these are combined into messages sent toward the central node. The visual emphasis suggests that the updated representation of one atom depends on the local chemical environment around it, which is the key idea behind graph-based learning on molecular structures.",
